    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Bottleneck 1 — Rigid Parallelism</h3>
<p>
  Wrapping both models in a single FSDP/ZeRO module forces the frozen target model through training-oriented synchronization. ZeRO-2 fully replicates the target on every GPU — tenable for 8B but impossible for 70B+ (OOM). ZeRO-3 amortizes memory by sharding, but triggers a full all-gather of target parameters at every forward pass, introducing synchronization latency that grows with model size. The Qwen3-235B-A22B ZeRO-3 baseline achieves only 2,026 tok/s because every training step is dominated by this communication.
</p>

<h3>Bottleneck 2 — Sub-optimal Prefill</h3>
<p>
  The target model's forward pass is structurally identical to a standard LLM prefill. But existing EAGLE-3 implementations use HuggingFace model classes that lack FlashAttention-3, CUDA Graphs, and chunked prefill. Even for the 8B case — where neither baseline nor SpecForge uses model parallelism — switching to SGLang's optimized inference runtime alone delivers a <strong>2.01× throughput gain</strong>. The SGLang backend outperforms HuggingFace by up to 6.8× across all tested models.
</p>

<h3>Bottleneck 3 — TTT Memory Pressure</h3>
<p>
  EAGLE-3's Training-Time Test (TTT) mechanism runs 7 autoregressive steps during training, simulating multi-token draft generation. At each step, the naive implementation materializes the full attention logit matrix as an intermediate activation. Over 7 TTT steps at sequence length 4096, these stored logits account for <strong>80% of total activation memory</strong> — more than the draft model's activations and parameters combined. This is the memory bottleneck that caps batch size and forces smaller effective throughput.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ─── §03 ─── -->
<section id="s-decouple" data-code="decouple">
<div class="section-no">03 — Target-Draft Decoupling</div>
<h2>SGLang as Target Backend: Up to 6.8× Prefill Speedup</h2>
<p>
  SpecForge's core architectural change is to route the target model through SGLang's inference engine rather than through HuggingFace or a custom implementation. SGLang brings piecewise CUDA Graph fusion (non-attention ops compiled into single kernels), FlashAttention-3 and FlashInfer (state-of-the-art attention kernels), tensor parallelism, expert parallelism, and pipeline parallelism — all of which are inference-optimized in a way that ZeRO-style training sharding cannot replicate.
</p>

